1
00:00:00,062 --> 00:00:01,569
A year ago, AI was a feature

2
00:00:01,569 --> 00:00:03,200
every SaaS product had to add,

3
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:04,538
like an assistant or an MCP

4
00:00:04,538 --> 00:00:05,199
server.

5
00:00:05,199 --> 00:00:07,784
Now it's the thing replacing them, and that's breaking the

6
00:00:07,784 --> 00:00:09,662
SaaS business model fast.

7
00:00:09,662 --> 00:00:11,508
Traditional SaaS has four parts.

8
00:00:11,508 --> 00:00:13,692
Building software used to be expensive and hard.

9
00:00:13,692 --> 00:00:15,784
Now coding agents make it nearly free.

10
00:00:15,784 --> 00:00:16,984
Running software at scale is

11
00:00:16,984 --> 00:00:18,338
expensive, demanding, and

12
00:00:18,338 --> 00:00:18,984
fragile.

13
00:00:18,984 --> 00:00:21,477
But most users run at a small scale, and that's now nearly

14
00:00:21,477 --> 00:00:24,276
free to answering users and fixing bugs used to take a

15
00:00:24,276 --> 00:00:25,692
support team and engineers.

16
00:00:25,692 --> 00:00:28,615
Now agents answer questions and fix bugs automatically.

17
00:00:28,615 --> 00:00:29,723
And the product vision.

18
00:00:29,723 --> 00:00:32,122
It comes from a founder and a team who obsess over a problem

19
00:00:32,122 --> 00:00:34,523
until they understand the solution better than anyone.

20
00:00:34,523 --> 00:00:35,846
That part hasn't changed.

21
00:00:35,846 --> 00:00:37,569
AI can't recreate taste.

22
00:00:37,569 --> 00:00:38,830
Buy versus build used to be a

23
00:00:38,830 --> 00:00:40,015
question only big companies

24
00:00:40,015 --> 00:00:40,553
asked.

25
00:00:40,553 --> 00:00:42,462
Now solo creators are asking it too.

26
00:00:42,462 --> 00:00:44,953
It's not only the monthly bill, it's paying for one hundred

27
00:00:44,953 --> 00:00:46,461
features to only use three.

28
00:00:46,461 --> 00:00:48,015
Building is becoming the default

29
00:00:48,015 --> 00:00:49,200
and building is becoming

30
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:49,877
cloning.

31
00:00:49,877 --> 00:00:52,707
Given agent access to a product and it copies the whole thing.

32
00:00:52,707 --> 00:00:55,046
But a clone only captures the tip of the iceberg.

33
00:00:55,046 --> 00:00:56,677
The vision, taste, constraints

34
00:00:56,677 --> 00:00:57,846
and opinions of the solution are

35
00:00:57,846 --> 00:00:59,385
lost and the user ends up with a

36
00:00:59,385 --> 00:01:00,245
worse version.

37
00:01:00,245 --> 00:01:03,138
They do it because they want three things ownership to use

38
00:01:03,138 --> 00:01:06,338
their own AI subscription and to shape it to their own needs.

39
00:01:06,338 --> 00:01:07,723
Introducing Super Grid, a

40
00:01:07,723 --> 00:01:09,262
desktop IDE for creators and

41
00:01:09,262 --> 00:01:09,968
builders.

42
00:01:09,968 --> 00:01:10,953
Here's how it works.

43
00:01:10,953 --> 00:01:13,753
First, you connect muse code through ACP so the agent runs on

44
00:01:13,753 --> 00:01:14,953
your own subscription.

45
00:01:14,953 --> 00:01:17,077
Then you connect your own Cloudflare account so everything

46
00:01:17,077 --> 00:01:18,738
is deployed and owned by you.

47
00:01:18,738 --> 00:01:21,477
Each workspace you create is its own code repo, and it ships with

48
00:01:21,477 --> 00:01:25,046
a database, events, workflows, analytics, and a media library.

49
00:01:25,046 --> 00:01:27,138
Apps are the main part of a workspace, and they're built on

50
00:01:27,138 --> 00:01:28,123
top of that core.

51
00:01:28,123 --> 00:01:29,138
You can build your own or

52
00:01:29,138 --> 00:01:30,308
install apps from other builders

53
00:01:30,308 --> 00:01:31,138
and founders.

54
00:01:31,138 --> 00:01:33,446
The app goes straight into your repo so you get their complete

55
00:01:33,446 --> 00:01:34,953
solution, not a clone.

56
00:01:34,953 --> 00:01:36,445
Apps can be internal tools like

57
00:01:36,445 --> 00:01:37,599
an ad designer built on muse

58
00:01:37,599 --> 00:01:38,738
image or deployed with your

59
00:01:38,738 --> 00:01:40,092
site, like a blog forms or

60
00:01:40,092 --> 00:01:40,985
landing pages.

61
00:01:40,985 --> 00:01:43,692
Each app can have its own data model, components, pages,

62
00:01:43,692 --> 00:01:45,107
routes, and back end code.

63
00:01:45,107 --> 00:01:46,769
It can add admin screens for your team.

64
00:01:46,769 --> 00:01:49,961
Hook into the core systems and expose its features over MCP, so

65
00:01:49,961 --> 00:01:51,577
muse can use the app natively.

66
00:01:51,577 --> 00:01:54,284
That makes every app a small product with its own front end

67
00:01:54,284 --> 00:01:56,531
and back end, built to fit into what's already there.

68
00:01:56,531 --> 00:01:57,745
The benefit of traditional SaaS

69
00:01:57,745 --> 00:01:59,022
is that updates and fixes are

70
00:01:59,022 --> 00:02:00,469
easy to ship, and everyone gets

71
00:02:00,469 --> 00:02:00,962
them.

72
00:02:00,962 --> 00:02:03,362
But with ownership, when everyone has their own copy,

73
00:02:03,362 --> 00:02:04,808
that ease disappears.

74
00:02:04,808 --> 00:02:06,684
Muse code is what makes this possible.

75
00:02:06,684 --> 00:02:09,177
It can extend apps, make changes, and merge updates from

76
00:02:09,177 --> 00:02:10,438
the builder seamlessly.

77
00:02:10,438 --> 00:02:12,653
It checks every update for security issues and fixes

78
00:02:12,653 --> 00:02:16,192
anything that breaks Muse code becomes the CTO for every user.

79
00:02:16,192 --> 00:02:18,931
It's what makes owning the code possible in this new SaaS model.

80
00:02:18,931 --> 00:02:21,238
Platforms like this could exist in every industry.

81
00:02:21,238 --> 00:02:23,700
Experts and founders could build solutions that get installed and

82
00:02:23,700 --> 00:02:25,053
owned instead of cloned.

83
00:02:25,053 --> 00:02:25,977
They'd focus on solving the

84
00:02:25,977 --> 00:02:26,838
problem, not on the

85
00:02:26,838 --> 00:02:28,100
infrastructure, and still get

86
00:02:28,100 --> 00:02:29,238
paid and build a real business

87
00:02:29,238 --> 00:02:30,069
around it.

88
00:02:30,069 --> 00:02:32,562
SaaS isn't going away, but it is changing.

89
00:02:32,562 --> 00:02:34,777
The code becomes part of the product that's shipped and owned

90
00:02:34,777 --> 00:02:35,762
by the user.

91
00:02:35,762 --> 00:02:38,162
Products built by the people who understand the problem best,

92
00:02:38,162 --> 00:02:40,838
shaped by the people who use them, and powered by the AI they

93
00:02:40,838 --> 00:02:41,792
already pay for.
